% composition-bridge: Hear how a Canadian indie-rock collective builds emotional scale through repetition, accumulating instruments, changing timbres and multiple musicians sharing an arrangement.
% composition-context: Broken Social Scene is a Toronto musical collective. The reference credits multiple writers and its arrangement is known for a repeated vocal idea surrounded by gradually expanding banjo, strings and band texture.
% composition-distance: Original D-mixolydian melody, lyric, counterline, metre changes and D-C-G harmony; no words, melody, banjo figure, string line, form or production detail from the reference is used.
% improv: {"id":"A","bars":"5-8","role":"ensemble","prompt":"Choose the homophonic colour","choices":["voices plus chords","instruments only","one voice with pedal tone"],"replyBars":4}
% improv: {"id":"B","bars":"13-16","role":"arranger/conductor","prompt":"Choose how the collective arrives","choices":["add one layer each bar","swap foreground and background","remove layers to one final voice"],"replyBars":4}
% dossier-function: original Grade 8 orchestral/collective indie-rock arranging bridge; students distribute melody, chords, pedal and counterline across available voices and instruments, then justify a live texture plan for the final four bars
% dossier-elements: sixteen bars, mainly 4/4 with 3/4 bars 4, 8, 12 and 16; D mixolydian D-E-F#-G-A-B-C, D4-B4 melody range; eighths, quarters, halves and changing bar length; D-C-G harmony over intermittent D pedal; monophony in bars 1-4, homophony in 5-8, imitative/polyphonic layers in 9-16; cumulative crescendo and through-built A-B-C-D arrangement
% dossier-practice: Rehearsal key D mixolydian; written melody range D4-B4 and counterline range A3-A4, with transposition or octave displacement allowed. Quarter note = 96. Rehearse the changing-metre phrase without accompaniment, assign one function per group, balance acoustic and electronic instruments, then have arrangers mark foreground, background, entry and exit on a shared chart.
% dossier-accompaniment: Newly authored D-C-G indie-rock frame for any ensemble: guitars or keys on chords, cello/bass on D pedal, percussion on a neutral backbeat and optional strings or winds on the counterline. Avoid the reference's recognizable banjo/string gestures and production arc.
% dossier-lyrics: Original English words about collective authorship. The lyric does not paraphrase the reference; students may replace the final two lines with jointly credited class text after consent and editorial review.
% dossier-honesty: AI-assisted original composition, 11 November 2026; not by Broken Social Scene and not an arrangement, cover or soundalike. The collective's recording is a case study in texture and ensemble authorship.
% dossier-licence: CC0 1.0 for supplied melody, words, harmony, counterline and ABC. New student text or arrangement decisions retain their authors' rights unless separately licensed; the reference remains copyrighted.
% dossier-respect: Credit the named collective and the song's listed writers rather than presenting its layered sound as anonymous "indie." Discuss whose ideas are foregrounded, credited and consented to when a large group creates together.
% dossier-review: Grade 8 teacher, indie-rock/recording educator, second score reader, student arranging group and copyright reviewer; all reviews still required
